<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Pakualaman</h1><p class="st-meta">1812 CE – present · capital Yogyakarta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Javanese princely house carved out of Yogyakarta in 1812, whose ruler today serves as the region's vice-governor.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>During <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-java">British Java</a>, Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paku-alam-i">Natakusuma</a>, a brother of the sultan of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yogyakarta-sultanate">Yogyakarta Sultanate</a>, sided with the British, who stormed the Yogyakarta kraton in 1812. As a reward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Raffles</a> granted him an apanage and the title Paku Alam I, creating an autonomous princely house modelled on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mangkunegaran">Mangkunegaran</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Paku Alam ruled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pura-pakualaman">Puro Pakualaman</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yogyakarta-sultanate">Yogyakarta</a> and a coastal district at Adikarto, holding a status below the sultan. The court was a centre of Javanese literature and arts, and the family produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ki-hajar-dewantara">Ki Hajar Dewantara</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taman-siswa">Taman Siswa</a> school movement and pioneer of Indonesian national education.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paku-alam-viii">Paku Alam VIII</a> supported the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Republic of Indonesia</a>, and when <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yogyakarta-sultanate">Yogyakarta</a> became a Special Region in 1950 its rulers became vice-governors alongside the sultan, an arrangement confirmed in 2012. Paku Alam X holds the post today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pakualaman.jpg" alt="Pakualaman" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ibrahim Muizzuddin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pakualaman.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1812 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yogyakarta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Paku Alam I, Paku Alam VIII, Paku Alam X</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kadipaten Pakualaman</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1919&amp;focus=pakualaman" aria-label="Pakualaman on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7732 2469 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pakualaman lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8052 2725L8053 2725M8052 2725L7959 2663M8052 2725L7959 2664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8053 2725h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8052 2725h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8052 2725h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1919&amp;focus=pakualaman">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
